  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 PM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nA strong upper ridge is over much of the Plains with the ridge axis \nextending from the Northern Plains to the Central Plains. An upper \nhigh is developing over portions of the Central and Southern Plains. \nIn our area, high temperatures have risen into the low 100s with \ndewpoints in the low 70s, resulting in heat indices exceeding 110 \nacross the area. As we continue into tonight and Monday morning, low \ntemperatures will remain 10-15 degrees above normal for this time of \nyear with lows near 80. The upper-level pattern will be consistent \nwith today for Monday as the upper ridge axis sits over the Plains \nwith an upper high expanding over much of the Central and Southern \nPlains. Above normal 850 mb temperatures are progged with the ECMWF \nshowing an 850 mb temperature anomaly between 5-8 C and the GFS \nbetween 8-13 C. While dewpoints will be slightly lower, they will \nlikely still range from the low 60s in central KS to low 70s in \nsoutheast KS. High temperatures will likely be warmer than today \nespecially for areas along and west of I-135, where its possible for \nthese highs to outperform the current forecast and be closer to 110. \nHeat indices will approach or exceed 110 across much of the area. As \nsuch, the Extreme Heat Warning has been extended through 9 PM \nMonday. \n\nThe upper ridge will break down Monday night into Tuesday as \nembedded shortwaves start to ride the upper ridging. A weak cold \nfront will push south through the Northern/Central Plains and into \nnorthern Kansas by Tuesday morning. Storms are progged to develop \noff the High Plains and move eastward into Nebraska and possibly \nnorthern Kansas during the overnight hours into early Tuesday \nmorning. Rain chances in central KS are around or below 25% with \nchances remaining very low for areas south of I-70. There is still \nuncertainty with the coverage of storms and how far south storms \nwill actually progress in Kansas due to very warm 700 mb \ntemperatures. Slightly cooler 850 mb temperatures and easterly \nsurface winds will result in high temperatures ranging from the mid \n90s in central KS to the lower 100s in southern KS. Dewpoints will \nreturn to the upper 60s to lower 70s, keeping heat indices between \n100-105 across much of the region. Some areas in south-central to \nsoutheast KS are likely to see heat indices exceed 105 degrees. As \nsuch, a Heat Advisory remains in effect through 8 PM Tuesday. \n\nAdditional rain chances will be possible late Tuesday night into \nWednesday morning as another weak shortwave trough slides across the \nregion with 700 mb WAA aiding in chances for widely scattered \nstorms. These chances are likely to linger across portions of \neastern KS on Wednesday morning with chances decreasing towards \nWednesday afternoon. Even with the upper ridge breaking down \nslightly over the Central Plains, high temperatures will remain in \nthe 90s across the region. Rain chances will again be possible \nduring the overnight hours on Wednesday night into Thursday morning, \nand then likely again Thursday night into Friday due to weak \nshortwaves being embedded in the northwesterly flow aloft. \nSevere weather chances with any of this activity continue to \nremain unlikely given the weak flow aloft over the Central \nPlains. The upper-level ridge axis will shift westward towards \nthe Central Great Basin and Desert Southwest late in the work \nweek, keeping much of the Plains in northwesterly flow aloft \nthrough next weekend.",
